WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:27.240
WHAT HAPPENED TO XEROX:
Have you ever heard of a company called Xerox?

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:30.340
Once upon a time, Xerox was a household name.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:35.690
In fact, they were even more than a household
name, they were a noun.

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:40.590
Whenever someone referred to a photocopy,
they would often call it a Xerox, and that’s

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:42.770
still the case to this day.

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:48.180
While photocopies was what Xerox was most
known for, this was by no means their only

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:49.180
contribution.

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:54.640
For example, they created a commercial computer,
the Xerox Alto, before Apple and even IBM,

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:57.400
having released it back in 1973.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:02.290
Xerox also created the first concept of a
laptop computer in 1976.

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:08.770
On top of this, Xerox even invented the ethernet
cable, and it was Xerox Altos that were used

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:10.540
to actually create the internet.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:14.510
And those are just Xerox’s most notable
contributions.

00:01:14.510 --> 00:01:19.591
Xerox is also credited with inventing graphical
user interfaces, laser printers, computer

00:01:19.591 --> 00:01:25.130
generated bitmap images, modern text editors,
and even object oriented programming.

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:29.619
And I think this statement from Bill Gates
when questioned about stealing from Apple

00:01:29.619 --> 00:01:31.380
really sums up the whole situation.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:36.850
“I think it’s more like we both had this
rich neighbor named Xerox and I broke into

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:42.680
his house to steal the TV set and found out
that you (Steve Jobs) had already stolen it.”

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:46.540
Given the historic significance of Xerox,
you would think that Xerox would be a trillion

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:52.710
dollar giant like Apple or Microsoft or atleast
a background giant like Oracle, but none of

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:54.330
this is the case.

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:59.750
In fact, after the dotcom bubble, Xerox stock
sold off and never recovered.

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:03.630
A similar story can be seen with their revenue
numbers as well.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:08.690
Within just the past 10 years, Xerox’s revenue
has fallen by 70%.

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:13.650
At least the company was still able to consistently
turn a profit, wait never mind, that just

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:15.050
changed as well.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:20.020
In the last 12 months, Xerox has literally
lost over a billion dollars.

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:25.720
And given that they only have $930 million
left in cash, it seems like a bankruptcy is

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:29.360
inevitable for the company, especially with
the recession.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:34.510
So, how did Xerox go from being a household
name responsible for leaps within the computer

00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:37.940
industry to balancing on the verge of bankruptcy?

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:44.440
THE RISE OF XEROX:
Taking a look back, the roots of Xerox date

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:49.340
back way further than any other tech company,
mostly because they weren’t even created

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:51.120
as a tech company.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:55.320
Xerox was established as a photographic paper
and equipment manufacturer in Rochester, New

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:56.569
York in 1906.

00:02:56.569 --> 00:03:02.320
Originally, they were named the Haloid Photographic
Company, and honestly, they didn’t do anything

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.560
noteworthy for the first 30 years.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:08.790
It appears that they were just a small company
that stayed a small company.

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:13.340
It wasn’t till 1938 that the tide would
start shifting for Xerox.

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:18.120
At the time, an independent physicist named
Chester Carlson was working on an invention

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:22.560
that could print images using an electrically
charged drum and toner.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:27.950
This invention caught the eye of Joseph R.
Wilson, the son of Xerox’s co founder.

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:32.659
Over the next several years, Joseph would
try to convince Chester to turn his invention

00:03:32.659 --> 00:03:34.340
into a commercial product.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:39.590
Chester would finally agree to do so in 1946,
and today, Chester Carlson is actually credited

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:41.170
as one of Xerox’ co founders.

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:46.940
At this point, Xerox finally had their hands
on a ground breaking product, but this was

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:48.480
only the beginning.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:53.220
They had to actually convince the public to
buy and/or use the darn thing, and that’s

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:56.200
exactly what Xerox would focus on over the
next few decades.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:59.159
The first thing they did was come up with
a name for the product.

00:03:59.159 --> 00:04:04.200
Initially, they would land on the term Xerography
which mean “dry writing” in greek.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:07.349
This would eventually evolve into being Xerox.

00:04:07.349 --> 00:04:11.879
The Haloid Company would officially debut
Xerography at the Optical Society of America

00:04:11.879 --> 00:04:14.390
on October 22, 1948.

00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:18.910
But, at this point, Xerography was still very
much just a concept.

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:24.210
While Xerox had a working prototype, there
was still a long way to go in making it commercially

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:25.370
viable.

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:29.530
That was ok though, this presentation was
mainly designed to simply generate and gauge

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:30.570
interest.

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:34.910
It appears that Xerox saw enough interest
for them to go ahead and trademark the term

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:37.000
“Xerox” that same year.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:41.810
Just one year later, Xerox would come out
with the Model A which became the world’s

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:43.410
first xerographic copier.

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:47.050
But, this too was by no means optimal.

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:50.940
Generating a single copy required multiple
steps, and the machine very much required

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:52.540
an expert to use.

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:57.070
But, Xerox kept iterating, and after another
10 years, they would create the world’s

00:04:57.070 --> 00:05:01.610
first automatic, plain-paper commercial copier
dubbed the Xerox 914.

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:06.280
That same year, they would create one of the
most iconic commercials of all time: a simple

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:11.530
commercial showcasing how easy it was to photocopy
a page from a book using the Xerox 914.

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:17.199
Nowadays, this seems like an ordinary day
in the life of an office worker, but back

00:05:17.199 --> 00:05:20.740
in 1959, this was truly a revolution.

00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:25.729
For the first time, people could photocopy
important documents and substantially lessen

00:05:25.729 --> 00:05:27.860
the burden of record keeping.

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:32.760
The Haloid Company very much knew what they
had created, and they were so confident in

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:37.930
the vision that they would change the name
of the entire compay to Xerox in 1961.

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:38.930
And the rest?

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:41.870
Well, the rest is history.

00:05:41.870 --> 00:05:47.539
PRINTING MONEY:
Xerox was never that intent on selling the

00:05:47.539 --> 00:05:49.410
914 itself.

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:53.620
They far preferred a renting model were users
would have to pay a fee for each photocopy.

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:59.539
They charged users $25 per month to get access
to a 914, and then they charged an additional

00:05:59.539 --> 00:06:01.160
10 cents per copy.

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:06.150
Customers also had to pay for ink and paper
as well which usually ran about 5 cents per

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:07.150
copy.

00:06:07.150 --> 00:06:11.750
So, the all in cost was $25 per month plus
15 cents per copy.

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:17.120
This translates to $256 per month plus $1.54
per copy.

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:22.250
The only person who didn’t rent the 914
was the US government who refused to rent.

00:06:22.250 --> 00:06:28.230
They wanted to buy 914s outright and the two
would eventually settle on a price of $27,500.

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:32.490
That’s $281,000 per machine in today’s
money.

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:38.819
Clearly, the 914 was by no means cheap, but
that didn’t prevent it from becoming extremely

00:06:38.819 --> 00:06:41.449
popular thanks to its usefulness.

00:06:41.449 --> 00:06:46.039
Within just 2 years of launch Xerox would
reach $60 million in revenue.

00:06:46.039 --> 00:06:50.479
Xerox would double down on the marketing and
make a commercial showcasing that even a chimpanzee

00:06:50.479 --> 00:06:52.040
could use the machine.

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:56.270
Around the same time Xerox would be listed
on the New York Stock Exchange which just

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:58.580
created even more hype for the company.

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:05.539
By 1965, they had pulled in over $500 million
in revenue which translates to $4.7 billion

00:07:05.539 --> 00:07:06.539
today.

00:07:06.539 --> 00:07:12.750
Xerox would follow up the 914 with several
iterations such as the 813, the 420, the 720,

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:14.789
the 330, and the 2400.

00:07:14.789 --> 00:07:18.819
All of these had slight variations and were
meant for slightly different purposes.

00:07:18.819 --> 00:07:24.830
For example, the 2400 was a duplicator as
opposed to a photocopier, but all of theses

00:07:24.830 --> 00:07:27.039
machines had the same end goal in mind.

00:07:27.039 --> 00:07:30.310
Make it cheaper and easier to reproduce documents.

00:07:30.310 --> 00:07:35.479
And given that this had become an essential
need for businesses and individuals alike,

00:07:35.479 --> 00:07:37.810
Xerox was literally printing money.

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:42.800
But, while everything at Xerox was going great,
Joseph Wilson was starting to get old.

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:48.410
He would step down from being CEO in 1967,
but he would serve as the chairman up until

00:07:48.410 --> 00:07:50.500
his death in 1971.

00:07:50.500 --> 00:07:55.710
Before he died, he would oversee one last
project called PARC or the Palo Alto Research

00:07:55.710 --> 00:07:56.710
Center.

00:07:56.710 --> 00:08:00.880
This is where all of the crazy inventions
that we talked about would come from: computers,

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:06.420
laptops, ethernet, the mouse, email, file
servers, and who knows what else.

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:10.220
It seemed like Xerox was very much planning
to pivot to the PC industry.

00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:14.120
They had the money, the expertise, and even
the technology.

00:08:14.120 --> 00:08:20.699
So, all they had to do was pivot and cash
in, but this pivot would never come.

00:08:20.699 --> 00:08:26.449
Instead, Xerox just pushed all of their inventions
under the rug and focused on their photocopiers.

00:08:26.449 --> 00:08:29.159
At first, this actually worked out fine.

00:08:29.159 --> 00:08:33.760
Xerox was expanding internationally, and they
were becoming a household name even in developing

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:34.760
countries.

00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:38.570
As we discussed at the beginning, Xerox was
basically synonymous with photocopies and

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:39.570
people would regularly refer to a photocopy
as a Xerox.

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:43.680
For Xerox, it probably seemed like nothing
could ever go wrong given that they had cornered

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:45.200
such an essential market.

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:50.560
But, as we now know, that was simply not the
case.

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:56.279
THE BRUTAL FALL:
Looking back, there was no pivotal moment

00:08:56.279 --> 00:08:58.020
that screwed over Xerox.

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:02.170
Rather, it was just decades of complacency
and ignorance.

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:08.440
I mean, outsiders understood Xerox’s potential
better than Xerox themselves, and I think

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:12.290
the best example of this is a story with Steve
Jobs.

00:09:12.290 --> 00:09:18.010
In 1979, Steve agreed to give Xerox somewhere
between 5-8% of Apple in return for a mere

00:09:18.010 --> 00:09:19.500
$1 million.

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:23.760
The condition for this investment though was
that Steve would get access to Xerox’s Palo

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:25.220
Alto Research Center.

00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:29.890
Steve would end up ripping off the Window,
Icon, Menu and Pointing Device systems from

00:09:29.890 --> 00:09:32.200
Xerox into new Apple computers.

00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:37.820
At this point, you can’t even blame Steve
for copying given that Xerox literally opened

00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:39.000
the doors for him.

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:43.660
Steve would later say quote “They just had
no idea what they had.”

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:47.700
You could say the same thing about the Apple
stake that they received as well.

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:51.960
Their stake would be worth a $150 billion
today.

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:55.839
Xerox could’ve atleast waited for Apple’s
IPO to sell the stake.

00:09:55.839 --> 00:10:01.110
But, instead, Xerox would end up flipping
the stake for a disgusting $1.2 million.

00:10:01.110 --> 00:10:04.980
Yeah, Xerox truly had no idea what they were
doing.

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:09.269
It seems that their leadership was just so
high on their success within the photocopying

00:10:09.269 --> 00:10:14.100
industry that they didn’t even care that
they were squandering such massive opportunities.

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:19.180
Eventually, Xerox would try to take a step
into the computer industry, but their attempt

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:21.510
was pathetic and ridiculous.

00:10:21.510 --> 00:10:26.570
In 1981, they would launch the Xerox Star
which was based on the Xerox Alto.

00:10:26.570 --> 00:10:28.870
The computer worked just fine.

00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:33.290
It had a nice user interface, respectable
hardware, and productive software.

00:10:33.290 --> 00:10:36.440
But, there was one major shortfall.

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:42.740
The darn thing cost $16,000 which translates
to over 50 gs today.

00:10:42.740 --> 00:10:48.579
Given that people could get a comparable machine
from IBM for less than $1,600, I don’t think

00:10:48.579 --> 00:10:52.440
you’d be surprised to hear that the Xerox
Star flopped like a fish out of water.

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:56.850
The worst part is that Xerox didn’t even
try to salvage the situation.

00:10:56.850 --> 00:11:00.660
They basically went: Oh well, we tried and
it didn’t work.

00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:02.010
Back to photocopying.

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:06.329
And I think this mindset is what really killed
Xerox.

00:11:06.329 --> 00:11:11.140
They were just so comfortable with photocopying
and it was so profitable that they didn’t

00:11:11.140 --> 00:11:12.740
even care to expand.

00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:17.410
This mindset was just made even worse by the
fact that they didn’t face any consequences

00:11:17.410 --> 00:11:18.990
for this behavior.

00:11:18.990 --> 00:11:21.389
In fact, they were being rewarded.

00:11:21.389 --> 00:11:27.269
As computers and the internet grew in popularity,
so did the need to print and photocopy documents.

00:11:27.269 --> 00:11:31.459
Combine this with the dotcom bubble and Xerox
stock would go vertical.

00:11:31.459 --> 00:11:39.550
Between 1982 and 1999, Xerox stock would rocket
900% despite making virtually no real progress.

00:11:39.550 --> 00:11:44.040
After the dotcom bubble burst, however, Xerox’s
house of cards would topple over and all that

00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:46.270
remained was the real Xerox.

00:11:46.270 --> 00:11:52.970
THE FATE OF XEROX:
In the end, if Xerox had just leveraged one

00:11:52.970 --> 00:11:56.940
of the many inventions that they helped create
whether that be the computers, servers, or

00:11:56.940 --> 00:11:59.779
the internet, they would be a mega giant today.

00:11:59.779 --> 00:12:04.680
But, instead, they’re on the brink of bankruptcy
with the same valuation that they were at

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:05.940
40 years ago.

00:12:05.940 --> 00:12:11.900
Oftentimes, we hear about corruption, greed,
and terrible reputations topple massive companies.

00:12:11.900 --> 00:12:17.510
But, when it comes to Xerox, the factor that
actually toppled them was simply too much

00:12:17.510 --> 00:12:18.630
success.

00:12:18.630 --> 00:12:22.900
The success blinded them from the need to
continue innovating and expanding.

00:12:22.900 --> 00:12:27.720
Instead, they just on their butts while their
empire crumbled from underneath them.

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:33.080
With the rise of electronic documents, esignatures,
and personal printers, people simply don’t

00:12:33.080 --> 00:12:35.860
need to go to Office Depot to make photocopies.

00:12:35.860 --> 00:12:40.380
In fact, if anything they’d prefer to back
up all their documents to the cloud instead

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:42.630
of keeping track of physical copies.

00:12:42.630 --> 00:12:47.720
At this point, Xerox is trying to sell an
outdated product to a shrinking market, and

00:12:47.720 --> 00:12:51.530
to be honest, there’s only one way this
ends: bankruptcy.

00:12:51.530 --> 00:12:54.339
But, that’s just what I think.

00:12:54.339 --> 00:12:58.389
Did you realize how much of our modern technologies
are attributable to Xerox?

